### Block 4 - persist() versus merge()
### Select the "local" environment. Needs at least one product (see 01-entities.http).

### merge() returns ANOTHER instance; persist() rejects detached objects
### Q: is the object we pass to merge() managed afterwards?
### Q: why does persist() fail on an object that already has an id?
GET {{host}}/demo/merge?productId={{productId}}

### See the current state of the product before the dangerous call
GET {{host}}/products/{{productId}}

### DANGER: PUT is built on merge(request.toEntity()).
### The body has no "stock": toEntity() turns the missing value into 0 and merge() OVERWRITES the real stock.
### Q: what is the stock after this call? Compare with the PATCH in 03-dirty-checking.http
PUT {{host}}/products/{{productId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Renamed by PUT"
}

### Check the damage
GET {{host}}/products/{{productId}}

### DANGER 2: PUT on an id that does not exist. merge() finds no row to update and fails
### with a misleading "Row was already updated or deleted" (500) instead of a clean 404.
PUT {{host}}/products/99999
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Ghost product",
  "stock": 1
}
